UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 34, Lesson 1: Choreographing a story dance', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Choreographing means making a dance. Choose a story, such as a growing seed or a journey, and plan the beginning, middle and end. Pick the movements and decide which elements, such as level, speed and energy, will change to show each part. Choose a formation if there is more than one dancer. Choose music or silence that matches the mood. Write a plan with the order of movements and counts. Then check that every choice helps tell the story. Take out movements that do not.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan a dance called ''The Snowflake'' with a beginning, middle and end. Say which element changes between parts.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Beginning: curled and still at a high level (falling). Middle: slow turns drifting across the floor (floating). End: landing low and still (melting). The speed changes and the level lowers.'' Each part matches the story and uses element changes.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that you must include as many movements as possible. A story dance is clearest when every movement has a reason, so choose a few strong ones.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoreograph a one-minute story dance. Write the title, plan with counts, music choice (or silence) and the elements that change. Mark one movement you may remove if it does not help the story, then rehearse.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Choreographing means making a dance. Choose a story, such as a growing seed or a journey, and plan the beginning, middle and end. Pick the movements and decide which elements, such as level, speed and energy, will change to show each part. Choose a formation if there is more than one dancer. Choose music or silence that matches the mood. Write a plan with the order of movements and counts. Then check that every choice helps tell the story. Take out movements that do not.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DAN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choreograph a one-minute story dance. Write the title, plan with counts, music choice (or silence) and the elements that change. Mark one movement you may remove if it does not help the story, then rehearse. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using choreography on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Beginning: curled and still at a high level (falling).. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible. A story dance is clearest when every movement has a reason, so choose a few strong ones. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating choreography on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with choreography, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about choreography with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for choreography. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for choreography. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Beginning: curled and still at a high level (falling).","Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material."],"question":"Plan a dance called ''The Snowflake'' with a beginning, middle and end. Say which element changes between parts. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for choreography; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.","Choreographing means making a dance.","''Beginning: curled and still at a high level (falling).","Careful use of a theme and explain why it matters with clear evidence."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with choreography?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a theme and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a theme and explain why it matters only, without linking it to choreography.","Apply choreography to new material and name the a theme and explain why it matters used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible."],"question":"Which response best applies choreography with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson on choreography. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.","''Beginning: curled and still at a high level (falling).","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which response best uses elements, techniques and audience effect for choreography?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.","Name a specific element or technique and say what effect it creates for choreography.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named."],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about choreography?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy another artwork’s sacred design without permission or guidance.","Example: Ana choreographs ''The Seed''."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Choreographing means making a dance. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue.","Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for choreography?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choreograph a one-minute story dance.","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.","Name a theme and explain why it matters only, with no linked example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies choreography to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about choreography with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating choreography on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using choreography on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for choreography. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with choreography, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Beginning: curled and still at a high level (falling).. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w34-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise choreography.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Dance","success_criteria":["I can use choreography with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a theme and explain why it matters.","I can practise a plan independently on a new prompt.","I can notice an element change needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Choreographing means making a dance.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Choreographing means making a dance."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about choreography with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Ana choreographs ''The Seed''. Beginning: curled low and still (seed). Middle: slow rising and stretching (growing), with arms opening as the sun comes out. End: tall, wide and strong with a big held shape (flower). She changes the level from low to high and the energy from heavy to light. A gentle slow piece of music helps the mood.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a dance called ''The Snowflake'' with a beginning, middle and end. Say which element changes between parts.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Beginning: curled and still at a high level (falling). Middle: slow turns drifting across the floor (floating). End: landing low and still (melting). The speed changes and the level lowers.'' Each part matches the story and uses element changes.","explain":"Choreographing means making a dance. Choose a story, such as a growing seed or a journey, and plan the beginning, middle and end. Pick the movements and decide which elements, such as level, speed and energy, will change to show each part. Choose a formation if there is more than one dancer. Choose music or silence that matches the mood. Write a plan with the order of movements and counts. Then check that every choice helps tell the story. Take out movements that do not.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that you must include as many movements as possible. A story dance is clearest when every movement has a reason, so choose a few strong ones. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Beginning and end of a dance.","caption":"Change the level.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"2